@normanmaurer @franz1981 
There is a possible null pointer risk when calling method `AdaptiveByteBuf.capacity(int newCapacity)` as I wrote in the review comment, add the following code before line:240 to the new PR class `AdaptivePoolingAllocator`:
```
// Simulate following call to 'mag.tryAllocate()' returned false.
if (buf != null) {
    break;
}
```
Then run following main code:
```
public static void main(String[] args) {
        ByteBufAllocator adaptiveAllocator = new AdaptiveByteBufAllocator();
        for (int i = 0; i < 128; i++) {
            if ( i == 124) {
                adaptiveAllocator.heapBuffer(1024 * 4 + 1);
                break;
            }
           adaptiveAllocator.heapBuffer(1024);
        }
        for (int i = 0; i < 128; i++) {
            if ( i == 120) {
                ByteBuf bf = adaptiveAllocator.heapBuffer(1024 * 4 + 5);
                bf.capacity(1024 * 4 + 6);
                return;
            }
            if (i == 119) {
                adaptiveAllocator.heapBuffer(1020);
            } else {
                adaptiveAllocator.heapBuffer(1024);
            }
        }
    }
```
